> the fact that a unicorn was grateful vs. gracious.

There are a few tricks for figuring out if a gem is valuable, but, as
you've discovered, that doesn't help if your goal is to sell them to a
shop.

For shopkeepers, you'll need the gem actually IDed.  You can save some
uses of identification by pre-identifying the valuable gems (using the
unicorn "trick" or other means of figuring out which gems are [likely
to be] valuable, including the amethyst trick and knowledge of
guaranteed valuable gems).

Beyond that, if you want to identify a lot of gems, hope you find a
touchstone.  When blessed, these will fully identify an unlimited
number of gems.

(Note that archaeologists and gnomes can use uncursed touchstones as
though they were blessed, and that archaeologists begin the game with
an uncursed touchstone.)


